# No `Homepage:` — there is no published home for this port or for the upstream Android app, and a
# URL invented to fill a required-looking field is worse than an absent one. Same decision, same
# reason, as `packaging/arch/PKGBUILD`'s empty `url=`.

Package: celeste-linux
Architecture: amd64
# The library dependencies are computed, not listed: `dh_shlibdeps` reads the `NEEDED` entries of
# everything in the app image, which is how `libxtst6`, `libxi6` and `libglvnd0` arrive — the same
# three that `namcap` found by hand on the Arch side, found here by the tool that owns the question.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends},
 pipewire-bin | pulseaudio-utils | alsa-utils,
 libnotify-bin,
 libglib2.0-bin,
 hicolor-icon-theme
Recommends: wireplumber, speech-dispatcher, espeak-ng
Description: voice assistant that talks to your own models
 Celeste listens, answers and speaks, on your machine and against your own
 models: speech in and speech out, chat, and image generation through a local
 ComfyUI. It is a port of the VoiceAssistant Android application to the Linux
 desktop, and it is deliberately quiet — no telemetry, no analytics, no update
 check, and no network call the user did not configure.
 .
 Speech recognition and the higher-quality voices come from a self-hosted Voice
 Gateway the user points it at; on-device voice activity detection (Silero VAD,
 via ONNX Runtime) decides when somebody is speaking, and eSpeak-NG or
 Speech Dispatcher can speak without any server at all.
 .
 It carries its own trimmed Java runtime, so no system JDK is needed, and it
 installs the same three things the desktop already knows how to read: an
 application menu entry, a hicolor icon, and a systemd *user* unit which is
 installed but deliberately not enabled.
